"1. Use LensKit 0.14.4 and implement exactly three recommenders: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop, with standard/default hyperparameters unless LensKit requires explicit equivalents.\n2. Load the three raw input files from the working directory using these exact filenames: u.data (MovieLens100K), VideoGames.csv (Amazon Video Games), and UserTaggedArtists-timestamps.dat (Last.FM).\n3. Parse each dataset into a user\u2013item interaction table with consistent user and item ID columns; preserve only the information needed for implicit-feedback recommendation.\n4. Apply 5-core filtering separately to each dataset so that every retained user and every retained item has at least 5 interactions after filtering converges.\n5. For MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games, convert ratings to implicit interactions by keeping only events with rating > 3 and discarding lower ratings before or during the 5-core preprocessing; treat retained events as binary interactions.\n6. For Last.FM, treat observed user\u2013artist/tag events as implicit interactions and deduplicate repeated user\u2013item events so evaluation uses binary relevance.\n7. Generate exactly 5 distinct random seeds for splitting and use the same seed set across all datasets and algorithms.\n8. For every dataset and seed, perform a user-based 80/20 holdout split so each user contributes interactions to both train and test when possible; ensure the split is reproducible from the seed.\n9. Exclude from evaluation any users who cannot support the holdout protocol after preprocessing/splitting (e.g., too few interactions), and report the number of evaluated users per dataset/seed.\n10. Train each algorithm separately on each dataset/seed training split using implicit-feedback data in the format expected by LensKit 0.14.4.\n11. Generate recommendations in batch for all evaluation users at once; do not use per-user recommendation loops.\n12. When scoring, filter out items already seen in each user\u2019s training data so only novel recommendations are evaluated.\n13. Compute Precision@1, Precision@5, Precision@10 and nDCG@1, nDCG@5, nDCG@10 for every dataset \u00d7 algorithm \u00d7 seed combination using the held-out test interactions as ground truth.\n14. Aggregate metrics correctly across users for each run, then summarize across the 5 seeds with mean and variability (at minimum standard deviation; preferably also confidence intervals).\n15. Conduct a short statistical analysis quantifying split-seed sensitivity, including within-dataset comparisons of metric variation across seeds for each algorithm and identifying which algorithms/datasets are most seed-sensitive.\n16. Organize results in a tidy tabular structure containing dataset, algorithm, seed, metric name, k, score, and evaluation-user count so downstream analysis is straightforward.\n17. Include basic validation and error handling for file loading, schema mismatches, empty datasets after filtering, and algorithms failing on sparse splits.\n18. Ensure the code is executable end-to-end in the working environment and produces both per-seed results and an overall concise analysis/report aligned with the research question."